  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y\\,y(3)=e.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=3$ to approximate $y(4).$",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(3)+{y}^{\u2032}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(3)=e.$ We also know that ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(3)=3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(e)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(3)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(3)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(3)+{y}^{\u2032}(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =e+(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =3x+e-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(4)$ using $L(4).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(4)\u2248L(4)=3(4)+e-9=e+3\n$$",
